<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Arrange the Chairs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in announcements or recordings. Interactive: Ask for repetition and clarification in a conversation about known topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify specific information in announcements about classroom tasks (e.g., "Team One sweeps the floor, and Team Two arranges the desks."). Can ask for repetition and clarification in conversations about classroom tasks (e.g., "Can you repeat which team cleans the board?" "Can you say that again? Who arranges the desks?"). Can identify specific details in recordings about classroom cleaning activities (e.g., "We sweep the floor.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher plays a short audio track featuring distinct classroom sounds like chairs scraping, a broom sweeping, a bucket being filled, and a whiteboard marker squeaking. Students listen and point to the corresponding real objects (chairs, mop, whiteboard) or flashcards displayed around the classroom. The teacher asks, "What do you hear?" to elicit simple responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards or realia (e.g., a small broom, a sponge, a toy chair) and clearly pronounces new vocabulary: "sweep," "wipe," "arrange," "chair," "desk," "mop," "sponge," "trash can." The teacher explicitly models the /r/ sound in "arrange" and the /s/ sound in "supplies" (introducing 'cleaning supplies'). Students listen and repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also demonstrates simple actions for verbs (e.g., pretends to sweep, wipe, arrange).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary by using visuals and simple questions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a broom picture) "Is this a pen or a mop?" (Expected: "A mop.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Performing a wiping action) "Am I sweeping or wiping?" (Expected: "Wiping.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (1-2 minutes) of a teacher giving simple classroom cleaning instructions and an announcement about team tasks. The language is simple and direct, using imperatives and the present continuous. Visual aids (pictures of classroom items and cleaning actions) are used in conjunction with the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example audio transcript excerpt: "Good morning, class! Listen carefully. Today, Team One is sweeping the floor. Team Two is arranging the chairs. Please put away the books. Can you help wipe the whiteboard, please? Let's keep our classroom tidy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in announcements or recordings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and then indicate, by raising hands or showing a "thumbs up/down," if the audio is about "playing games" or "cleaning the classroom."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Information (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students are given a worksheet with simple True/False statements based on the audio. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Team One is wiping the window." (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Team Two is arranging the chairs." (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We are sweeping the floor." (T)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen to the audio one more time. On their worksheet, they match teams to tasks (e.g., "Team One" to "sweep the floor," "Team Two" to "arrange the chairs"). The teacher also asks students to listen for compound words like "whiteboard" and identify the two syllables (white-board).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, identifying present continuous (e.g., "We are sweeping"), imperatives (e.g., "Sweep."), and modal "can" (e.g., "Can you help me?"). Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, whiteboard, sweep, wipe, arrange, tidy, clean, please.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher plays sentences with a missing key vocabulary word or action verb. Students listen and write the missing word on a pre-printed cloze worksheet. They choose from a given word bank. E.g., Audio: "Please ____ the floor." (Word bank: wipe, sweep). Audio: "The classroom is ____." (Word bank: messy, tidy). This activity includes vocabulary like 'quickly', 'carefully', 'quietly' in sentence context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen and Identify):</b> The teacher plays short audio clips of instructions (e.g., "Sweep the floor."). Students listen and identify the action being performed or requested. The teacher then asks, "What are we doing?" or "What should we do?" Students listen to the teacher's question and respond with the target verb or simple phrase. This also focuses on identifying the /s/ sound in "sweeps" and the /r/ in "arranges".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen for Repetition/Clarification):</b> In pairs, students take turns being "Teacher A" and "Student B." Teacher A gives a simple instruction related to classroom tasks (e.g., "Please arrange the desks."). Student B listens. If Student B didn't understand, they use target phrases to ask for repetition or clarification: "Can you repeat, please?" or "Can you say that again? Who arranges the desks?" Teacher A then repeats or clarifies. This activity practices both giving instructions ("Please arrange the chairs.") and asking for clarification, integrating politeness with "please".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-play / Simulation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of three. The teacher plays a recording of a longer, more complex classroom announcement assigning multiple cleaning tasks to different 'teams' (e.g., "Attention, class! Team A, you are sweeping the floor quickly. Team B, please wipe the tables carefully. And Team C, arrange the chairs quietly. Don't forget the trash can. Can I help you with anything?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After listening to the announcement twice, each group discusses the tasks. One student in the group (Student 1) pretends to be the "Team Leader" and tries to recall and re-state the instructions heard in the announcement to the other two students (Student 2 and Student 3). Student 2 and Student 3 act as "Team Members" and must actively listen. If they don't understand a task or a specific detail (e.g., which team does what, or which item to clean), they must ask Student 1 for repetition or clarification (e.g., "Can you repeat which team cleans the board?" "Can you say that again? Who arranges the desks?"). Student 1 then clarifies, using the target vocabulary and grammar, incorporating "please" for politeness. Student 2 can also offer help to Student 3 by saying "Can I help you sweep?" if they identify a task. The focus is on active listening, extracting information, and using interactive phrases for clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher makes a final "live" announcement with three distinct classroom cleaning instructions (e.g., "Listen everyone! Please arrange the chairs. Then, sweep the floor. Finally, wipe the whiteboard."). Students listen and draw three simple pictures representing each instruction in order, or write down the key action word for each instruction (e.g., 1. arrange, 2. sweep, 3. wipe).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b> The teacher observes students during the Production stage and the final assessment task, noting:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify specific information from the announcements/recordings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of phrases for asking repetition and clarification (e.g., "Can you repeat?", "Can you say that again?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of key vocabulary (e.g., sweep, wipe, arrange, chair, desk, whiteboard, trash can).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in collaborating and listening to peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target /r/ and /s/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to listen for _____ (e.g., instructions/cleaning words)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned is _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask for help/repeat by saying _____."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for me to hear and understand today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult for me to hear or understand? How can I improve my listening?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The listening activities generally worked well. Students were engaged with the audio and visual aids, and the opportunity to interact and ask for clarification was valuable. The pronunciation focus on /r/ and /s/ needs continuous reinforcement. Some students still struggled to distinguish between "sweep" and "wipe" purely by sound, requiring more visual reinforcement. The integration of "please" was natural in the instruction-giving tasks. For the next lesson, I need to ensure continued exposure to these core vocabulary words and phrases in a reading context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson introduced key vocabulary and listening strategies related to classroom cleaning. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will reinforce these same vocabulary words and concepts through a short reading text, allowing students to see the words in written context and further develop their comprehension of the "Taking Care of Our Class" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>